.TH BLITZCTL 1 "2026-09-09" "0.14.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
blitzctl \- control utility for the apt-blitz on-disk cache
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B blitzctl
[\fI\,OPTIONS\/\fR] \fI\,COMMAND\/\fR [\fI\,ARGS\/\fR]
.br
.B blitzctl cache
\fI\,SUBCOMMAND\/\fR [\fI\,ARGS\/\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBblitzctl\fR is a companion utility for the \fBapt-blitz\fR HTTP forward
proxy.  It inspects and manages the on-disk cache directly against the
SQLite database, so it can be used while the proxy is running.
.P
The cache directory is resolved the same way as the service: the YAML
\fIcache_dir\fR option, then the \fBPROXY_CACHE_DIR\fR environment variable,
then the compiled-in default \fB/var/cache/apt-blitz\fR.  The
.B \-\-cache\-dir
option overrides it.
.P
Every command accepts a \fIHOST\fR argument that may be a configured
.B \-\-url\-map
alias or the real upstream host.  Filtering matches either form, and output
is shown in the matching alias/real perspective.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-cache\-dir \fI\,PATH\/\fR
Cache directory (overrides config/env default).
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print help information and exit.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH CACHE SUBCOMMANDS
.SS hosts
List cached resource hosts with totals (alias, real host, size, file count).
.br
Usage: \fBblitzctl cache hosts\fR
.SS tree
Show the per-host resource filesystem tree.  Individual files are hidden by
default; use \fB\-\-files\fR/\fB\-f\fR to list them.
.br
Usage: \fBblitzctl cache tree\fR \fIHOST\fR [\fIPATH\fR]
.br
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-files
Also list individual cached files.
.SS find
Search files and folders within a host by name.  \fB*\fR matches any run of
characters and \fB?\fR matches any single character; the match is
case-insensitive.  A \fB/\fR in the query matches against the full path,
otherwise the match is against the name (last component).
.br
Size and age filters narrow the result to matching files; folders are only
shown when they contain at least one file that passes the filters.
.br
Usage: \fBblitzctl cache find\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIHOST\fR \fIQUERY\fR
.TP
.B \-\-min\-size \fI\,SIZE\/\fR
Minimum file size: plain bytes or a \fBk/m/g/t\fR suffix standing for
powers of 1024, e.g. \fB1024\fR, \fB1k\fR, \fB2M\fR, \fB1G\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-size \fI\,SIZE\/\fR
Maximum file size, with the same suffix syntax as \fB\-\-min\-size\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-cached\-min\-age \fI\,DURATION\/\fR
Only entries cached at least \fIDURATION\fR ago (based on the time the
entry was first cached).  Follows \fBfind(1)\fR semantics: \fB1d\fR keeps
entries older than one day.
.TP
.B \-\-cached\-max\-age \fI\,DURATION\/\fR
Only entries cached within \fIDURATION\fR of now (e.g. \fB30m\fR, \fB6h\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-access\-min\-age \fI\,DURATION\/\fR
Like \fB\-\-cached\-min\-age\fR, but based on the last access time.
.TP
.B \-\-access\-max\-age \fI\,DURATION\/\fR
Like \fB\-\-cached\-max\-age\fR, but based on the last access time.
.P
A duration is a number with an optional \fBs/m/h/d/w\fR suffix (seconds,
minutes, hours, days, weeks); a bare number means seconds.
.SS ls
List cached entries like the system \fB\&ls\fR over the cache URL namespace,
optionally limited to \fIHOST\fR and a \fIPATH\fR prefix or glob.  Both take
the form \fBblitzctl cache ls\fR [\fIHOST\fR] [\fIPATH\fR]; when \fIHOST\fR
is omitted all cached hosts are listed.  \fIHOST\fR and \fIPATH\fR are
separate positional arguments: URLs and single\-token \fBhost/path\fR
selectors are rejected.
.br
Usage: \fBblitzctl cache ls\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIHOST\fR] [\fIPATH\fR]
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-long
Long format: cached date, last access, seconds until expiry, size.
.TP
.B \-\-human
Human-readable sizes (e.g. \fB1.0 MiB\fR).
.TP
.B \-R, \-\-recursive
Recurse into subdirectories.
.TP
.B \-1, \-\-oneline
Print one entry per line (default when output is not a terminal).
.TP
.B \-N, \-\-name
Sort by name (default).
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-time
Sort by last access time (newest first).
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-cached
Sort by cache time (\fIcached_at\fR, newest first).
.TP
.B \-S, \-\-size
Sort by size (largest first).
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-reverse
Reverse the sort order.
.P
Directories are always listed first, regardless of the sort key.
.SS info
Show details of a single cached file: URL, host/path in the active
perspective, size, file checksums (\fBMD5\fR, \fBSHA1\fR, \fBSHA256\fR,
\fBSHA512\fR), cached time, last access, freshness (seconds until expiry),
content type, and the stored file location.  The checksums are computed on
the fly from the stored file and are marked \fB\- (stored file missing or
unreadable)\fR when the file cannot be read.
.br
Usage: \fBblitzctl cache info\fR \fIHOST\fR \fIPATH\fR
.SS cat
Print the raw contents of a single cached file to stdout (streamed, without
loading it into memory).  Like \fBinfo\fR, takes an unambiguous \fIHOST\fR +
\fIPATH\fR pair; glob patterns are not supported and a selector that matches
zero or more than one entry is an error.  Nothing but the file bytes is
written to stdout.
.br
Usage: \fBblitzctl cache cat\fR \fIHOST\fR \fIPATH\fR
.SS cp
Copy a single cached file to the local filesystem, using the same
unambiguous \fIHOST\fR + \fIPATH\fR selector as \fBinfo\fR.  If \fIDEST\fR
is an existing directory, the file is placed inside it under its original
name; otherwise \fIDEST\fR is used as the literal file path and its parent
directory must already exist.  Refuses to overwrite an existing file unless
\fB\-\-force\fR/\fB\-f\fR is given.  After the copy the byte count is
checked against the cached size and a warning is printed on mismatch.
.br
Usage: \fBblitzctl cache cp\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIHOST\fR \fIPATH\fR \fIDEST\fR
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-force
Overwrite the destination file if it already exists.
.SS rm
Remove cached entries: all entries, or only those matching \fITARGET\fR
(\fBhost\fR or \fBhost/path\fR, prefix or exact file).  Removing the entire
cache asks for confirmation (type \fByes\fR) unless \fB\-\-yes\fR is given.
.br
Usage: \fBblitzctl cache rm\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fITARGET\fR]
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt (full removal only).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
List cached hosts:
.RS
.B blitzctl cache hosts
.RE
.TP
Browse a host's filesystem tree including files:
.RS
.B blitzctl cache tree
\fIdeb.debian.org\fR
.B \-\-files
.RE
.TP
Search for \fI.deb\fR packages on a host:
.RS
.B blitzctl cache find
\fIdeb.debian.org\fR
.B "*\.deb"
.RE
.TP
Search for large packages cached over two weeks ago:
.RS
.B blitzctl cache find
\fIdeb.debian.org\fR
.B "*\.deb"
.B \-\-min\-size
\fI10M\fR
.B \-\-cached\-min\-age
\fI2w\fR
.RE
.TP
List the largest cached entries under a path:
.RS
.B blitzctl cache ls
.B \-S
\fIsecurity.debian.org\fR
\fIpool\fR
.RE
.TP
Show details of a single cached file:
.RS
.B blitzctl cache info
\fIdeb.debian.org\fR
\fIpool/main/a/apt_1.0_all.deb\fR
.RE
.TP
Print the raw contents of a cached file to stdout:
.RS
.B blitzctl cache cat
\fIdeb.debian.org\fR
\fIpool/main/a/apt_1.0_all.deb\fR
.RE
.TP
Copy a cached file to the current directory (overwrite with \fB\-\-force\fR):
.RS
.B blitzctl cache cp
\fIdeb.debian.org\fR
\fIpool/main/a/apt_1.0_all.deb\fR
\fI.\fR
.RE
.TP
Remove cached entries for a host selector:
.RS
.B blitzctl cache rm
\fIdeb.debian.org\fR
.B \-\-yes
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/cache/apt-blitz
Default cache directory (contains the \fBcache.db\fR SQLite database and the
sharded cached files).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR apt-blitz (1)